// A CPU stand-in for <metal_stdlib>, so tessl's Metal kernels can be compiled
// as C++20 and executed on a host with no Metal toolchain (Linux CI, a cloud
// container) — see tools/msl_emu/README.md.
//
// What it models:
//
//   * a threadgroup is `threads_per_threadgroup` real OS threads, and
//     `threadgroup_barrier` is a real barrier across them;
//   * a simdgroup is 32 consecutive threads, and every `simd_*` collective is a
//     real exchange through shared slots with a 32-way barrier, so a lane that
//     skips a collective its siblings reach deadlocks here as surely as it
//     misbehaves on a GPU;
//   * `simdgroup_matrix<float, 8, 8>` is held whole by every lane (a real GPU
//     spreads it across lanes, but the arithmetic is the same), and a store
//     writes only the lane's own two elements, as a GPU's does — so a missing
//     `simdgroup_barrier` between a store and another lane's read is a race
//     here too, not a silent pass.
//
// What it does not model: memory-model reorderings, threadgroup-memory limits
// beyond what the harness allocates, fast-math precision, and anything a
// kernel in this repo does not use. A pass here is evidence the indexing,
// masking, barrier placement and algebra are right — not a substitute for
// running on the device.
